1
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:54,935
And we're on in four, three,

2
00:00:54,935 --> 00:00:57,057
two, one.

3
00:00:59,013 --> 00:01:02,848
What if I told you that even the
worst neighborhood in America

4
00:01:02,848 --> 00:01:05,126
could be made completely safe.

5
00:01:05,126 --> 00:01:08,124
And what if I told you that
this could be accomplished

6
00:01:08,124 --> 00:01:12,440
without risking the life of one
single law enforcement officer.

7
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:13,959
How do I know this?

8
00:01:13,959 --> 00:01:20,598
Because it's happening right now in every
country in the world but this one.

9
00:01:20,993 --> 00:01:24,672
I am Pat Novak, and welcome
to "The Novak Element".

10
00:01:29,146 --> 00:01:32,902
In tonight's State of the Union segment
we will be joined by General Curtis Monroe

11
00:01:32,902 --> 00:01:35,308
live from the War Room in the Pentagon.

12
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:39,936
General Monroe, welcome back.
Good to see you, Pat.

13
00:01:39,936 --> 00:01:44,452
Thank you for this unprecedented
access to your operations.

14
00:01:44,452 --> 00:01:50,566
Could you walk us through what we're seeing
here? Well, this is a routine operation.

15
00:01:50,566 --> 00:01:56,241
We found that random patrols helped the local
population to feel safer and to trust us.

16
00:01:56,241 --> 00:02:01,397
It's amazing. And what are these colored
dots that we're seeing here, General?

17
00:02:01,397 --> 00:02:04,434
These are our assets on the ground.

18
00:02:04,434 --> 00:02:07,072
In green, we got the ED-209s.

19
00:02:07,072 --> 00:02:09,949
They're the big boys.
They secure the perimeter.

20
00:02:09,949 --> 00:02:13,906
Purple are the smaller EM-208s.
They sweep and mop up.

21
00:02:13,906 --> 00:02:16,863
And the red dots, Pat,
that's your film crew.

22
00:02:18,543 --> 00:02:24,457
It is great to see American machines
helping to promote peace and freedom abroad.

23
00:02:24,457 --> 00:02:27,734
All right, you guys, this is a standard op,
all right? There's no opposition expected.

24
00:02:27,734 --> 00:02:30,140
I am going to set your
markers to code red.

25
00:02:33,290 --> 00:02:35,887
All our drones are going to give
top priority to your safety.

26
00:02:35,887 --> 00:02:40,848
No red assets have ever been lost. Okay?
Okay. Does that make you feel better?

27
00:02:40,848 --> 00:02:44,559
But, listen, keep those bracelets on,
you're completely safe.

28
00:02:44,559 --> 00:02:47,158
Okay? Good. Enjoy the show.

29
00:02:52,873 --> 00:02:55,437
All right. Let's go.

30
00:03:00,226 --> 00:03:02,506
May peace be upon you.

31
00:03:02,904 --> 00:03:08,778
Please exit your homes with your arms
raised for a non-invasive scanning procedure.

32
00:03:08,778 --> 00:03:11,025
No one will be harmed.

33
00:03:20,968 --> 00:03:26,334
Please exit your homes with your arms raised
for a non-invasive scanning procedure.

34
00:03:26,643 --> 00:03:28,480
No one will be harmed.

35
00:03:30,959 --> 00:03:32,808
Scott, get me a shot of this.

36
00:03:33,317 --> 00:03:38,557
Your co-operation is appreciated.
We apologize for any inconvenience.

37
00:03:40,551 --> 00:03:42,353
May peace be upon you.

38
00:03:50,262 --> 00:03:51,508
Incredible.

39
00:03:51,821 --> 00:03:58,214
Incredible. Not long ago, that would
have been American men and women

40
00:03:58,214 --> 00:04:01,575
risking their lives to pacify these people.

41
00:04:02,452 --> 00:04:04,768
General, you care to comment on this?

42
00:04:04,768 --> 00:04:10,295
Well, Pat, we had Vietnam,
we had Iraq, we had Afghanistan.

43
00:04:10,644 --> 00:04:15,559
Never again. From a military
perspective, this has been invaluable.

44
00:04:15,559 --> 00:04:19,476
We can accomplish our objectives
without any loss to American lives

45
00:04:19,476 --> 00:04:22,995
and I think the honest people
over there really appreciate it.

46
00:04:22,995 --> 00:04:24,791
And why wouldn't they?

47
00:04:24,791 --> 00:04:29,627
For the first time in their lives they
get to watch their children grow up

48
00:04:29,627 --> 00:04:33,715
in an environment of safety and security.

49
00:04:35,079 --> 00:04:35,537
Navid!

50
00:04:35,537 --> 00:04:36,996
Get away from the window!

51
00:04:37,329 --> 00:04:39,163
Mom! They're here! They're coming!

52
00:04:40,413 --> 00:04:41,997
Come here! Come here right now!

53
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:43,164
Arash!

54
00:04:44,414 --> 00:04:46,205
Navid! You stay with your mom and sisters!

55
00:04:46,205 --> 00:04:47,080
But he's just a kid.

56
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:47,497
Seven!

57
00:04:48,038 --> 00:04:49,497
Stay here with your mom and the kids!

58
00:04:50,123 --> 00:04:51,706
What do you think you're doing?
You heard me!

59
00:04:51,956 --> 00:04:54,331
Arash, please don't go! Don't do this!

60
00:04:57,415 --> 00:04:58,582
Navid! My dear! Come back here!

61
00:05:00,249 --> 00:05:00,790
Navid!

62
00:05:01,499 --> 00:05:02,332
Shut the door!

63
00:05:03,750 --> 00:05:05,332
We're not here to kill any of them.

64
00:05:06,249 --> 00:05:06,833
Got it?

65
00:05:07,333 --> 00:05:10,041
The goal is to die on television.

66
00:05:10,584 --> 00:05:11,041
Let's go!

67
00:05:12,310 --> 00:05:15,227
Kelly.
Can you hear me out there?

68
00:05:15,227 --> 00:05:18,144
Loud and clear, Pat.
I am here in sunny Tehran...

69
00:05:18,144 --> 00:05:22,460
...where locals have clearly
embraced these routine scans.

70
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:26,417
As you can see behind me, they're working
in collaboration with the robots

71
00:05:26,417 --> 00:05:31,097
and obviously their safety
is everyone's top priority.

72
00:05:48,796 --> 00:05:50,338
Don't make a move before I say...

73
00:05:50,796 --> 00:05:52,547
I wanna make sure they're recording.

74
00:05:53,130 --> 00:05:53,505
Yes!

75
00:05:53,881 --> 00:05:54,380
Yes! Yes!

76
00:05:55,006 --> 00:05:55,589
We're ready.

77
00:06:01,147 --> 00:06:06,621
You will notice children in the streets as well.
This is everyday life happening in perfect harmony.

78
00:06:11,778 --> 00:06:14,455
Oh, great. Here, come here.
Stay put, stay put, stay put.

79
00:06:14,455 --> 00:06:16,255
It's fine. Don't worry.
My guy's got it.

80
00:06:30,260 --> 00:06:31,260
Navid! It's dangerous here!

81
00:06:31,260 --> 00:06:32,093
Come away! Come...

82
00:06:32,343 --> 00:06:33,594
Navid, where are you going?

83
00:06:35,135 --> 00:06:36,552
Look at me! Navid! Navid!

84
00:06:36,552 --> 00:06:38,011
What are you looking for? Navid!

85
00:06:39,011 --> 00:06:40,595
Navid! Navid! What are you...

86
00:06:41,052 --> 00:06:42,052
Navid! Don't go! Navid!

87
00:06:58,617 --> 00:06:59,977
Oh, my God.

88
00:07:06,730 --> 00:07:08,214
Drop your weapon.

89
00:07:13,284 --> 00:07:16,560
Kelly?
Kelly. Are you all right? Kelly.

90
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:20,359
We're okay, Pat. Our robots have just
repelled some suicide bombers. Hold on, Kelly.

91
00:07:20,597 --> 00:07:25,712
I am sorry, Kelly, but for security reasons
the Pentagon's going to cut our feed to Tehran.

92
00:07:25,712 --> 00:07:29,279
But, please, be careful.
Yeah. I will continue to stand by.

93
00:07:31,508 --> 00:07:36,266
Now you see how fundamental these
robots are to our foreign policy.

94
00:07:36,743 --> 00:07:42,662
So then tell me. Why can't we use
these machines here at home?

95
00:07:42,662 --> 00:07:47,775
Why is America so robophobic?

96
00:07:48,493 --> 00:07:51,694
On the one hand, we have Raymond Sellars.

97
00:07:52,409 --> 00:07:59,053
An entrepreneur and true American whose
product just saved my colleague's life.

98
00:07:59,053 --> 00:08:01,281
And on the other.

99
00:08:01,281 --> 00:08:03,798
Hubert Dreyfus.

100
00:08:03,798 --> 00:08:08,046
A man with both feet
planted firmly in the past

101
00:08:08,046 --> 00:08:12,471
whose bill prohibits the
use of drones on US soil.

102
00:08:12,471 --> 00:08:15,668
The American people
support the Dreyfus Act

103
00:08:15,668 --> 00:08:22,552
because they haven't experienced this technology
and they fear what they don't understand.

104
00:08:22,943 --> 00:08:26,541
We at "The Novak Element"
support Mr. Sellars' efforts

105
00:08:26,541 --> 00:08:29,935
to make our country and our viewers safer.

106
00:08:29,935 --> 00:08:36,181
After all, what's more important than
the safety of the American people?

107
00:09:03,187 --> 00:09:04,990
- Hey, Murphy.
- Hey.

108
00:09:07,024 --> 00:09:08,633
How's Jack?

109
00:09:08,633 --> 00:09:10,511
We don't know yet.

110
00:09:10,781 --> 00:09:14,700
- Hey. Murphy.
- How's it going? Good.

111
00:09:20,252 --> 00:09:24,647
What the hell are they doing here?
Six in the morgue. Lewis in the hospital.

112
00:09:24,647 --> 00:09:28,684
This was the mother of all bad ideas.
We got a lead on Antoine Vallon, we took it.

113
00:09:28,684 --> 00:09:29,885
What lead?

114
00:09:30,643 --> 00:09:32,128
Not in front of them.

115
00:09:32,442 --> 00:09:36,197
This is bullshit. Yeah?
Yeah, you got something to say, Murphy?

116
00:09:36,197 --> 00:09:41,400
Vallon's our case. Yeah, two years without no arrest,
no nothing? You have got to be dumb or dirty.

117
00:09:42,193 --> 00:09:46,878
You got something on us? Or is this
personal? No, it's nothing personal.

118
00:09:47,109 --> 00:09:52,795
Although, I don't like you as a person.
Okay, you two, out. No problem.

119
00:09:54,023 --> 00:09:57,179
Maybe if you hadn't gone off the reservation,
your partner would still be standing.

120
00:09:57,179 --> 00:09:59,418
- What'd you say to me?
- Hey, that's enough.

121
00:10:02,135 --> 00:10:03,540
Okay, Alex.

122
00:10:06,132 --> 00:10:08,139
It's just you and me now.

123
00:10:08,690 --> 00:10:12,891
So me and Jack, we have been doing some
street buys. We get a line on some guns.

124
00:10:13,446 --> 00:10:16,522
We meet up with this
low-level G called Jerry

125
00:10:16,522 --> 00:10:19,160
who ends up having a piece
he's not supposed to have.

126
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:21,118
Where the hell you get a
gun like that, Jerry?

127
00:10:21,118 --> 00:10:24,275
I can get you two whole crates full, man.
Two crates?

128
00:10:24,275 --> 00:10:27,352
No disrespect, J, but how a clown
like you get so much product, man?

129
00:10:27,352 --> 00:10:28,671
What's it matter where it comes from?

130
00:10:28,671 --> 00:10:31,429
Why it matters, we don't
want no dirty guns, Jerry.

131
00:10:31,429 --> 00:10:34,586
These guns got bodies on them, Jerry?
Yo, look, they're clean, all right?

132
00:10:34,586 --> 00:10:37,703
You going to buy this shit or not?
Hey, hey. Why you acting so nervous, man?

133
00:10:37,703 --> 00:10:40,621
You fishing on us, Jerry?
What'd you say?

134
00:10:40,621 --> 00:10:42,859
- You think he fishing, D?
- I think he's fishing on us.

135
00:10:42,859 --> 00:10:45,902
Yo, what the hell are they talking about,
man? Hey, let me see your phone.

136
00:10:46,177 --> 00:10:48,574
Let me see your phone.
I am not showing you my phone, man.

137
00:10:48,574 --> 00:10:51,481
You recording this? What?
I think you're a goddamn cop.

138
00:10:51,481 --> 00:10:54,129
What the hell are you talking about,
man? I am not showing you my phone.

139
00:10:54,129 --> 00:10:56,567
Hey. Put your gun down.
Put your phones on the table.

140
00:10:56,567 --> 00:10:59,844
We're not cops, all right?
We work for Antoine Vallon. What? Hey, hey.

141
00:10:59,844 --> 00:11:03,960
Shut up. Shut up. What'd you say?
Ends up he works for Vallon.

142
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:06,957
Before the meet, we ran the
serial numbers on the rifles.

143
00:11:06,957 --> 00:11:09,318
The guns were supposed to
be in our evidence room.

144
00:11:10,356 --> 00:11:14,271
Not out on the street.
Intake papers blank. No signatures.

145
00:11:14,271 --> 00:11:16,711
- What do you got?
- Nothing.

146
00:11:17,349 --> 00:11:19,391
How do you think that happened?

147
00:11:21,106 --> 00:11:23,263
I am telling you, Murphy,
this is a mistake.

148
00:11:23,263 --> 00:11:26,385
These guys will shoot you just
to see if they gun work.

149
00:11:26,621 --> 00:11:27,819
We need back-up.

150
00:11:27,819 --> 00:11:31,742
If we call for backup, word's going to get
around the station. He's going to disappear.

151
00:11:31,742 --> 00:11:33,580
It's going to be all right.

152
00:11:34,175 --> 00:11:37,930
So we told Vallon we were gun
buyers, and he bought it.

153
00:11:37,930 --> 00:11:42,053
What do you two need with that
much product? You plotting a coup?

154
00:11:42,547 --> 00:11:45,364
Man, don't trip, we ain't going to
bust a single shot in your town.

155
00:11:45,364 --> 00:11:47,362
We got some problems back
home with Chicago PD.

156
00:11:47,883 --> 00:11:50,609
Yeah, I heard you don't like cops.

157
00:11:50,1000 --> 00:11:52,563
Hey, boss.

158
00:11:53,877 --> 00:11:55,566
What do you got for me?

159
00:11:57,154 --> 00:12:01,190
Really? We were just about to close the
deal but someone tipped him off.

160
00:12:01,190 --> 00:12:05,398
Gentlemen, I got to deal with this.
Two minutes.

161
00:12:12,261 --> 00:12:13,905
Make sure you kill them both.

162
00:12:22,652 --> 00:12:25,659
I got two handguns, one automatic.

163
00:12:38,118 --> 00:12:42,046
We got to get out of here. I am going
to take the automatic. You sure about this?

164
00:13:04,175 --> 00:13:05,375
You ready?

165
00:13:05,375 --> 00:13:06,654
Move.

166
00:13:46,978 --> 00:13:48,338
Lewis.

167
00:13:49,056 --> 00:13:50,381
Lewis.

168
00:13:53,053 --> 00:13:56,888
Officer down.
Officer down, 331 Hamilton.

169
00:13:56,888 --> 00:14:02,686
This is Detective Alex Murphy in need of
immediate medical assistance. 331 Hamilton Road.

170
00:14:03,483 --> 00:14:04,763
Jack.

171
00:14:05,562 --> 00:14:08,729
Jack, come on.
Jack. Can you hear me?

172
00:14:09,038 --> 00:14:11,205
Jack. Stay with me.

173
00:14:11,956 --> 00:14:13,236
Jack.

174
00:14:13,634 --> 00:14:18,869
Alex, you don't do something like
this unless you bring it to me first.

175
00:14:18,869 --> 00:14:24,344
Vallon's connected. I am warning you,
don't make any more moves until I vet this.

176
00:14:24,344 --> 00:14:28,423
If officers are supplying him guns...
That's up to IAD.

177
00:14:28,661 --> 00:14:30,385
I will handle it.

178
00:14:35,855 --> 00:14:40,693
I don't care how sophisticated
these machines are, Mr. Sellars.

179
00:14:40,693 --> 00:14:45,366
A machine does not know what
it feels like to be human.

180
00:14:45,366 --> 00:14:49,442
It can't understand the value of human life.
Why should it be allowed to take one?

181
00:14:50,562 --> 00:14:57,995
To legislate over life and death, we need
people who understand right from wrong.

182
00:14:57,995 --> 00:15:03,989
What do your machines feel? Well, they feel
no anger. They feel no prejudice.

183
00:15:03,989 --> 00:15:08,748
They feel no fatigue, which makes
them ideal for law enforcement.

184
00:15:09,545 --> 00:15:16,059
Putting these machines on the streets
will save countless American lives.

185
00:15:16,059 --> 00:15:19,176
You're evading the question. No, I am not.
Yes, you're evading the question.

186
00:15:19,176 --> 00:15:25,855
I asked what do these machines feel?
If one of them killed a child,

187
00:15:26,730 --> 00:15:28,248
what would it feel?

188
00:15:31,806 --> 00:15:33,843
- Nothing.
- And that's the problem.

189
00:15:33,843 --> 00:15:39,238
That's why 72% of Americans will not
stand for a robot pulling the trigger.

190
00:15:45,953 --> 00:15:50,313
America is the world's most valuable
market, and we can't touch it.

191
00:15:50,313 --> 00:15:55,864
With a domestic expansion, we'd
clear $600 billion per annum.

192
00:15:55,864 --> 00:16:00,899
In other words, every second that law is
in existence, we are hemorrhaging money.

193
00:16:00,899 --> 00:16:01,858
What do you want me to do?

194
00:16:01,858 --> 00:16:05,575
We have thrown cash at senators at both
sides of the aisle. Nobody's budging.

195
00:16:05,575 --> 00:16:07,653
They don't want to vote
against their constituents.

196
00:16:07,653 --> 00:16:12,969
So unless we sway public opinion,
the battle's lost. Now, to that effect,

197
00:16:12,969 --> 00:16:17,405
my team have come up with a
pretty aggressive new campaign.

198
00:16:17,405 --> 00:16:23,085
Wow factor off-the-charts. We need to
give Americans a product they can love.

199
00:16:23,085 --> 00:16:28,235
A figure they can rally behind. Sir, I have
dissected this bill. There are no loopholes.

200
00:16:28,235 --> 00:16:32,917
We can't put a machine on the streets.
Forget machines. You saw the polls.

201
00:16:33,192 --> 00:16:35,597
Americans don't want a machine.

202
00:16:36,629 --> 00:16:41,270
They want a product with a conscience.

203
00:16:41,704 --> 00:16:46,830
They want something that knows

204
00:16:47,659 --> 00:16:51,905
what it feels like to be human.

205
00:16:58,250 --> 00:17:00,769
We're going to put a man
inside a machine.

206
00:17:04,364 --> 00:17:10,038
You're not you because of your legs, your arms,
your hands. You're you because of your brain.

207
00:17:10,038 --> 00:17:14,638
It's your brain's capacity to process
information that makes you who you are.

208
00:17:15,834 --> 00:17:20,274
Relax, and just do what
you have always done.

209
00:17:23,867 --> 00:17:25,904
I have never played with these.

210
00:17:25,904 --> 00:17:30,341
The music doesn't come from your fingers,
Michael, it comes from your mind.

211
00:17:30,341 --> 00:17:32,508
All you have to do is let it out.

212
00:17:33,259 --> 00:17:34,619
Please.

213
00:18:05,231 --> 00:18:06,919
Oh, my God.

214
00:18:11,026 --> 00:18:12,828
No, it's okay. Easy.

215
00:18:14,743 --> 00:18:18,900
His emotions are peaking. The readings
are getting clouded. It's okay.

216
00:18:19,339 --> 00:18:23,814
Relax. If you get too emotional, it will
change the chemistry in your brain.

217
00:18:23,814 --> 00:18:25,777
It will throw the system out.

218
00:18:28,650 --> 00:18:31,374
You're doing great. Come on.

219
00:18:45,835 --> 00:18:47,956
I need emotion to play.

220
00:18:48,593 --> 00:18:50,919
Concierto de Aranjuez, second movement.

221
00:18:52,070 --> 00:18:56,676
Beautiful piece.
Dennett, when you have a moment. Thank you.

222
00:18:56,676 --> 00:18:59,782
Now, we agreed on this a
long time ago, Raymond.

223
00:18:59,782 --> 00:19:03,659
I wouldn't be asked to
develop combat applications.

224
00:19:03,659 --> 00:19:06,896
My work here is driven by its own questions.

225
00:19:06,896 --> 00:19:10,533
And your work is brilliant.
But your ideas are being underutilized.

226
00:19:10,533 --> 00:19:17,733
Look, I think I found a way to put real
revenue stream behind your research and

227
00:19:17,733 --> 00:19:21,087
save thousands of lives in the process.

228
00:19:33,713 --> 00:19:36,670
You're not keeping up your end,
leaving me exposed like that.

229
00:19:36,670 --> 00:19:39,787
Detective Murphy's just a little impulsive.
So put him on the payroll.

230
00:19:39,787 --> 00:19:40,946
Why are we talking about this?

231
00:19:40,946 --> 00:19:44,663
It won't work. And now that his partner
got hit, he's never going to let it go.

232
00:19:44,663 --> 00:19:46,701
- What is that?
- Lewis' hospital.

233
00:19:46,701 --> 00:19:50,381
Alex is dropping by this afternoon,
in case you want to send flowers.

234
00:19:54,455 --> 00:19:58,970
Yeah. I kill a cop, looking over my
shoulder the rest of my life.

235
00:19:58,970 --> 00:20:00,501
That's not good for business.

236
00:20:01,089 --> 00:20:03,574
Who do you think's going to
be investigating you?

237
00:20:09,562 --> 00:20:12,080
I am going to go hard after
Lake and Daniels.

238
00:20:14,078 --> 00:20:16,801
I am going to have them in bracelets
within a month, man.

239
00:20:18,434 --> 00:20:20,433
Hey, Vallon, I am walking up right now.

240
00:20:21,231 --> 00:20:22,841
Cameras off?

241
00:20:23,989 --> 00:20:25,189
Clear.

242
00:20:26,307 --> 00:20:31,627
You were right.
We should have had back-up. I am sorry.

243
00:20:34,939 --> 00:20:36,981
You're not really sleeping, are you?

244
00:20:39,375 --> 00:20:40,700
Asshole.

245
00:20:42,892 --> 00:20:47,889
Sergeant Thomas Ryan, NYPD.
Paralyzed from the waist down.

246
00:20:48,248 --> 00:20:53,602
He has let himself go a
little since the accident. Pass.

247
00:20:53,602 --> 00:20:55,247
Yeah. Yeah, fair enough.

248
00:20:55,601 --> 00:20:58,245
Officer Alfred Miller, Denver.

249
00:20:58,559 --> 00:21:03,638
"Severe Cerebellar..."
Cerebellar ataxia.

250
00:21:03,638 --> 00:21:05,398
That's why you're the doctor, Doctor.

251
00:21:05,752 --> 00:21:09,508
He scored through the roof
with our urban demos.

252
00:21:09,508 --> 00:21:11,346
Let's keep this one in mind.
I like this guy.

253
00:21:11,346 --> 00:21:14,276
Very good, sir.
I saved the best for last.

254
00:21:15,584 --> 00:21:19,579
I give you Sergeant Jack
Freeman, masculinity incarnate.

255
00:21:19,579 --> 00:21:24,498
Led Pittsburgh SWAT for five years,
now confined to a wheelchair.

256
00:21:25,035 --> 00:21:30,010
I... think I can see the appeal. No.

257
00:21:30,010 --> 00:21:35,125
What? Why? Well, check the psych evaluation.
He's far too unstable.

258
00:21:35,125 --> 00:21:37,134
He's trying to say that he has a temper.

259
00:21:37,134 --> 00:21:39,761
Have you any idea what we'd
be putting him through?

260
00:21:39,761 --> 00:21:45,396
The psychological strain requires someone
emotionally balanced. We have to be scientific.

261
00:21:45,396 --> 00:21:51,203
I mean, we're not selling a soft drink here,
are we? He's right. Okay. Keep looking.

262
00:21:52,911 --> 00:21:58,188
David, what did I say? Not until
you're finished. Hey, guys. Hi, Dad.

263
00:21:58,188 --> 00:22:01,150
- Hey.
- Hey, little monkey.

264
00:22:05,180 --> 00:22:09,615
How's the game, buddy? Damon got ejected,
but we got a two-man advantage.

265
00:22:09,615 --> 00:22:12,233
Oh, yeah? That's fantastic.

266
00:22:12,233 --> 00:22:16,015
Hey, D, why don't you head upstairs
and start getting ready for bed.

267
00:22:16,015 --> 00:22:17,853
But he just got home.

268
00:22:18,289 --> 00:22:22,364
I will be up in a second.
Tuck you in, okay? All right?

269
00:22:26,122 --> 00:22:29,605
- Now give your mom a kiss.
- Good night, baby. Too slow, boy.

270
00:22:31,437 --> 00:22:33,205
You're just too slow.

271
00:22:40,909 --> 00:22:44,905
How's Jack holding up?
They said a week till he's back on his feet.

272
00:22:45,585 --> 00:22:48,187
It's my fault.
We shouldn't even have been there.

273
00:22:50,660 --> 00:22:51,860
Here.

274
00:23:55,923 --> 00:23:59,366
Oh, shit, I thought I fixed that.
It will stop.

275
00:24:05,434 --> 00:24:07,397
It's going to wake up David.

276
00:24:50,914 --> 00:24:55,638
He suffered 4th degree burns
over 80% of his body.

277
00:24:55,990 --> 00:24:58,634
Lower spine has been severed.

278
00:24:59,387 --> 00:25:01,508
If he survives,

279
00:25:02,065 --> 00:25:06,152
he will be paralyzed from the waist
down and confined to a wheelchair.

280
00:25:08,699 --> 00:25:10,626
Mrs. Murphy,

281
00:25:12,176 --> 00:25:14,695
we don't have to do this.

282
00:25:15,853 --> 00:25:19,099
I need to know everything
before I make a decision.

283
00:25:22,167 --> 00:25:23,855
Please continue.

284
00:25:24,645 --> 00:25:30,123
Vestibulocochlear, optical nerves
have been critically damaged.

285
00:25:30,959 --> 00:25:33,285
He will be blind in one eye.

286
00:25:33,717 --> 00:25:35,360
Most likely deaf.

287
00:25:36,195 --> 00:25:40,110
Frankly, Dr. Norton and the Omni
Foundation are the leaders in this field.

288
00:25:40,110 --> 00:25:46,467
In my opinion, this is the only chance your
husband has. I'd seriously consider their offer.

289
00:25:49,063 --> 00:25:50,548
I am sorry, Doctor.

290
00:25:51,941 --> 00:25:57,621
I need time to think. I am afraid, Mrs.
Murphy, time is one thing you don't have.

291
00:26:01,452 --> 00:26:04,494
You say you can save him,
but what does that mean?

292
00:26:07,567 --> 00:26:09,733
What kind of life will he have?

293
00:26:19,117 --> 00:26:21,602
# Fly me to the moon #

294
00:26:22,034 --> 00:26:25,952
# Let me play among the stars #

295
00:26:26,470 --> 00:26:30,906
Thanks. Thanks, buddy.
You know she's going to make me dance again.

296
00:26:30,906 --> 00:26:33,903
Now that I have got to see.
You don't want to see that.

297
00:26:33,903 --> 00:26:37,640
Here she comes now. Hold on to that.
Get out there, partner.

298
00:26:39,739 --> 00:26:41,666
Every time.

299
00:26:45,294 --> 00:26:46,492
Get her, boy.

300
00:26:51,449 --> 00:26:54,005
# Fill my heart with song #

301
00:26:54,005 --> 00:26:58,571
# And let me sing forever more #

302
00:26:59,402 --> 00:27:04,836
# You are all I long for #
# All I worship... #

303
00:27:04,836 --> 00:27:06,874
Subject is still dreaming.

304
00:27:08,154 --> 00:27:13,436
He's retrieving memories, trying to make
sense of sensorial stimulation package 37.

305
00:27:14,229 --> 00:27:15,952
Perfect, Kim.

306
00:27:17,266 --> 00:27:19,466
Let's reconnect the spine.

307
00:27:20,263 --> 00:27:21,987
Time to wake him up.

308
00:27:25,419 --> 00:27:28,455
# Fill my heart with song #

309
00:27:28,455 --> 00:27:32,260
# Let me sing forever more #

310
00:27:33,412 --> 00:27:36,648
# You are all I long for #

311
00:27:36,648 --> 00:27:41,169
# All I worship and adore #

312
00:27:41,564 --> 00:27:43,844
# In other words #

313
00:27:44,162 --> 00:27:49,962
# Please be true #
Excuse me?

314
00:27:50,397 --> 00:27:52,199
What are you doing here?

315
00:27:52,199 --> 00:27:57,190
Baby, where you going?
# In other words #

316
00:27:57,190 --> 00:28:02,135
Lewis.
# I love........ #

317
00:28:05,943 --> 00:28:07,473
# You #

318
00:28:08,980 --> 00:28:12,386
Subject regaining cognitive functions.

319
00:28:14,056 --> 00:28:15,586
What's happening?

320
00:28:17,773 --> 00:28:23,527
Where am I? You were in an explosion,
Alex. You're in a hospital.

321
00:28:24,047 --> 00:28:31,040
Where is... Where's Clara? My wife. Where's my
wife? I want you to calm down, Alex. Listen to me.

322
00:28:31,040 --> 00:28:34,799
You're okay. And your family's fine.
Both your wife and your son.

323
00:28:35,477 --> 00:28:36,921
Who the hell are you?

324
00:28:36,921 --> 00:28:40,278
I am Dr. Dennett Norton.
I am treating you.

325
00:28:40,278 --> 00:28:42,037
"Heart rate" rising.

326
00:28:44,549 --> 00:28:46,159
I can't move.

327
00:28:48,546 --> 00:28:51,262
I can't move.
What the hell is all that?

328
00:28:51,262 --> 00:28:54,060
Visual input unstable.
I want you to relax, Alex.

329
00:28:54,060 --> 00:28:57,103
Subject's dumping too much epinephrine.
Should we purge it? Not yet.

330
00:28:57,538 --> 00:28:59,738
Unlock vertebrae.

331
00:29:04,532 --> 00:29:06,061
Go ahead, Kim.

332
00:29:06,450 --> 00:29:09,651
Unlock upper, lower extremities.

333
00:29:22,116 --> 00:29:24,353
What kind of suit is this?

334
00:29:24,353 --> 00:29:28,078
It's not a suit, Alex.

335
00:29:29,989 --> 00:29:31,428
It's you.

336
00:29:33,146 --> 00:29:38,709
This was the only option. We're here,
all of us, to make you whole again.

337
00:29:39,101 --> 00:29:42,977
Full body unlock.
I want you to walk now, Alex.

338
00:29:42,977 --> 00:29:44,780
You can do that.

339
00:30:08,316 --> 00:30:10,278
This seems so real.

340
00:30:12,991 --> 00:30:18,066
Alex, it's very important you understand
what is happening. This is real.

341
00:30:18,066 --> 00:30:21,423
It's not a dream,
it's not drugs or anesthetics.

342
00:30:21,423 --> 00:30:24,340
I know I am dreaming because
I can feel all of this.

343
00:30:24,340 --> 00:30:28,817
Alex, amputees can often feel their missing
appendages. It's called phantom limb sensation.

344
00:30:28,817 --> 00:30:30,302
What do you mean "amputees"?

345
00:30:30,536 --> 00:30:33,752
What the hell did you do to me?
You need to relax. Take this off me now.

346
00:30:33,752 --> 00:30:36,697
You need to relax. I need to get out
of this thing. What did you do to me?

347
00:30:36,697 --> 00:30:38,818
What have you done to me?

348
00:30:39,248 --> 00:30:41,336
Alex? Hey.

349
00:30:42,486 --> 00:30:46,408
Shut him down.
No. Let him run. Let him run.

350
00:31:06,824 --> 00:31:08,263
Where am I?

351
00:31:32,601 --> 00:31:34,290
Alex, please.

352
00:31:35,759 --> 00:31:37,118
Stop.

353
00:31:37,877 --> 00:31:39,884
Alex, you have to stop.

354
00:31:41,114 --> 00:31:42,553
Please.

355
00:31:43,472 --> 00:31:44,797
Alex.

356
00:31:46,150 --> 00:31:47,793
Please stop.

357
00:31:48,108 --> 00:31:52,708
Stay where you are and we will
come and pick you up.

358
00:32:26,035 --> 00:32:27,678
Okay, Kim.

359
00:32:28,752 --> 00:32:30,190
Shut him down.

360
00:32:56,008 --> 00:32:57,732
Alex,

361
00:32:58,206 --> 00:33:00,247
you can't run from this.

362
00:33:02,043 --> 00:33:06,449
You have to understand the
reality of the situation.

363
00:33:09,836 --> 00:33:11,275
Show me.

364
00:33:13,233 --> 00:33:14,638
Kim.

365
00:33:30,458 --> 00:33:31,607
Oh...

366
00:33:34,055 --> 00:33:35,301
No.

367
00:33:39,370 --> 00:33:41,059
Holy Christ.

368
00:33:41,848 --> 00:33:43,326
Holy Christ.

369
00:33:45,365 --> 00:33:47,884
Holy Christ, there's nothing left.

370
00:33:53,797 --> 00:33:57,680
Your body may have gone,
but you're still here.

371
00:34:03,948 --> 00:34:09,103
That's not even my brain.
We had to repair the damaged areas.

372
00:34:09,103 --> 00:34:15,066
But we didn't interfere with your emotion or
your intellect. Do you understand me, Alex?

373
00:34:15,578 --> 00:34:18,541
You're in control.

374
00:34:19,135 --> 00:34:21,779
- I am in control?
- Yes.

375
00:34:24,331 --> 00:34:25,815
Okay.

376
00:34:28,767 --> 00:34:31,331
If I am in control,

377
00:34:32,483 --> 00:34:34,446
then I want to die.

378
00:34:35,721 --> 00:34:41,207
Just unplug whatever it is keeping
me alive and end this nightmare.

379
00:34:41,515 --> 00:34:43,213
Now, say I did that.

380
00:34:43,213 --> 00:34:48,351
Which as a doctor would almost be
impossible for me, but say I did.

381
00:34:50,028 --> 00:34:52,467
What do I say to your wife?

382
00:34:52,865 --> 00:34:57,512
What does she say to your son?
That it didn't work. That you tried.

383
00:34:58,341 --> 00:35:00,223
Something went wrong.

384
00:35:00,898 --> 00:35:04,100
You did everything you could, but,

385
00:35:06,693 --> 00:35:13,809
I died. So after all they have been
through, all the pain, all their hope restored,

386
00:35:13,809 --> 00:35:16,133
we would just rip that away?

387
00:35:17,484 --> 00:35:19,384
Your wife loves you, Alex.

388
00:35:19,384 --> 00:35:21,839
She signed the consent forms herself.

389
00:35:21,839 --> 00:35:25,359
Otherwise, you couldn't have
undergone the procedure.

390
00:35:35,308 --> 00:35:40,146
She loves you and she
gave you a second chance.

391
00:35:42,182 --> 00:35:44,951
I need you to take it.

392
00:35:49,136 --> 00:35:51,905
I don't want to see
myself like this again.

393
00:35:53,292 --> 00:35:56,851
Ever.
And the same goes for my family.

394
00:36:04,323 --> 00:36:06,330
Just put me back in.

395
00:36:10,677 --> 00:36:11,957
Zoom in.

396
00:36:14,154 --> 00:36:15,514
Stop.

397
00:36:18,230 --> 00:36:19,635
Zoom in.

398
00:36:21,747 --> 00:36:23,311
Stop.

399
00:36:28,222 --> 00:36:30,104
Accept the connection.

400
00:36:35,256 --> 00:36:40,178
It's so good to finally see you.
I was going to say the same thing.

401
00:36:41,130 --> 00:36:42,774
I am sorry.

402
00:36:43,528 --> 00:36:48,605
I know how I must look to you.
I think you look beautiful.

403
00:36:52,281 --> 00:36:59,004
Are they treating you okay, baby? So far.
Dr. Norton, he seems like a nice guy.

404
00:37:00,234 --> 00:37:02,514
Does David know you're calling?

405
00:37:05,749 --> 00:37:07,551
He misses you.

406
00:37:08,107 --> 00:37:10,433
- I can go get him.
- No.

407
00:37:11,544 --> 00:37:13,949
I don't want him to see me like this.

408
00:37:15,940 --> 00:37:17,220
Not yet.

409
00:37:17,220 --> 00:37:18,578
Sure.

410
00:37:24,252 --> 00:37:26,976
We're going to get through this, baby.

411
00:37:29,448 --> 00:37:32,047
We're going to make it like it was.

412
00:37:36,402 --> 00:37:40,605
When are you coming home?
Well, they have still got some tests.

413
00:37:41,477 --> 00:37:43,803
But they're saying it's going really well.

414
00:37:45,514 --> 00:37:47,202
The minute they let me.

415
00:37:49,430 --> 00:37:51,154
I got to go.

416
00:37:52,108 --> 00:37:55,395
I love you.
End the connection.

417
00:38:09,533 --> 00:38:13,575
Okay, blood cleansing initiated.

418
00:38:15,168 --> 00:38:18,971
Viral and bacterial screening activated.

419
00:38:21,003 --> 00:38:24,598
Releasing proteins, fats,

420
00:38:24,598 --> 00:38:27,607
minerals and carbs.

421
00:38:29,595 --> 00:38:34,796
Energy inflow is at 1320 kilocalories.

422
00:38:36,189 --> 00:38:43,627
Now starting anti-inflammatory corticoids,
hormones and antibiotic protocols.

423
00:38:43,982 --> 00:38:46,262
Standard daily intake.

424
00:38:48,498 --> 00:38:50,824
Release antidepressants.

425
00:38:51,776 --> 00:38:54,294
Let's give him a nice dream.

426
00:39:05,244 --> 00:39:09,679
All right, here's what you're dealing with.
Modified M2 battle rifle.

427
00:39:09,679 --> 00:39:14,205
.50 caliber Beowulf ammunition at 30 round
clips. Three-shot bursts or full auto.

428
00:39:15,315 --> 00:39:16,561
Heavy-duty.

429
00:39:17,673 --> 00:39:20,989
TSR 66. Cartridge-driven taser pistol.

430
00:39:20,989 --> 00:39:27,076
Each projectile releases up to 200,000 volts.
It can stun, it can kill and it's nasty shit.

431
00:39:27,076 --> 00:39:29,861
Your armor, state-of-the-art,
but not invulnerable.

432
00:39:29,861 --> 00:39:32,579
.50 caliber or larger
can penetrate and kill.

433
00:39:32,579 --> 00:39:36,462
Multiple hits, and guess who
ends up in the recycling bin?

434
00:39:38,614 --> 00:39:40,702
If you sense you need a
weapon or are threatened

435
00:39:42,491 --> 00:39:45,767
the visor goes down and the system
makes the weapons available.

436
00:39:45,767 --> 00:39:48,724
But you got to remember,
it's not the weapons that count.

437
00:39:48,724 --> 00:39:52,283
It's who's handling them.
Or, in your case, what.

438
00:39:52,283 --> 00:39:55,119
- You think you can do this?
- Oh, just stick to your job.

439
00:39:55,119 --> 00:39:58,116
Just stick to my job?
Combat robots are my job, Doc.

440
00:39:58,116 --> 00:40:02,353
Rio, Congo, you name it, I was there.
Our machines run perfectly.

441
00:40:02,353 --> 00:40:06,752
You ask me, sticking organics into
the system is a huge step backwards.

442
00:40:07,029 --> 00:40:09,107
I don't care how well you
designed this thing.

443
00:40:09,107 --> 00:40:13,154
Without my sign-off, it better start learning
Chinese, because it ain't never going home.

444
00:40:20,498 --> 00:40:22,585
It's nice to meet you, too.

445
00:40:23,775 --> 00:40:25,577
Let's do this,

446
00:40:25,577 --> 00:40:27,178
Tin Man.

447
00:40:33,326 --> 00:40:40,604
Quality control, EM-208 versus Tin Man.
Rendering scenario one now.

448
00:40:41,239 --> 00:40:44,517
Hey, Doc, hope you don't mind.
I like a little music while I am working.

449
00:40:45,476 --> 00:40:48,272
# When a man's an empty kettle #

450
00:40:48,272 --> 00:40:53,430
All right. All right, let's let the
games begin. Let's rock 'n' roll.

451
00:40:54,747 --> 00:40:57,584
Commence scenario.
# Just because I am presuming #

452
00:40:57,584 --> 00:40:59,542
# That I could be kind of human #

453
00:40:59,542 --> 00:41:03,149
# If I only had a heart #

454
00:41:04,059 --> 00:41:09,174
# I'd be tender, I'd be gentle #
# And awful sentimental #

455
00:41:09,174 --> 00:41:11,818
# Regarding love and art #

456
00:41:13,011 --> 00:41:15,528
# I'd be friends with the sparrows #

457
00:41:15,528 --> 00:41:17,766
# And the boy who shoots the arrows #

458
00:41:17,766 --> 00:41:20,855
# If I only had a heart #

459
00:41:22,563 --> 00:41:26,446
# Picture me a balcony #

460
00:41:27,279 --> 00:41:32,274
# Above a voice sings low... #
Lagging on the corners.

461
00:41:32,274 --> 00:41:35,960
No, there, d'you see that?
What my robot did, that's how you do it.

462
00:41:38,069 --> 00:41:39,474
New product's afraid.

463
00:41:40,148 --> 00:41:43,712
2.3 seconds behind. Great.

464
00:41:45,063 --> 00:41:47,708
Don't shoot. He has a gun.
Drop your gun.

465
00:41:49,939 --> 00:41:51,139
Drop your gun.

466
00:41:51,897 --> 00:41:53,575
Drop the weapon. Let the kid go.

467
00:41:53,575 --> 00:41:58,621
Oh, well, it's worried that the kid's going
to get hurt. Let the kid go. Too bad.

468
00:41:59,970 --> 00:42:04,086
Well, there you have it. 5.6
seconds behind. Scenario failure.

469
00:42:04,086 --> 00:42:06,174
I wouldn't buy that for a dollar.

470
00:42:11,400 --> 00:42:14,044
- Let's do it again.
- Your funeral.

471
00:42:17,635 --> 00:42:18,873
Detective Alex Murphy.

472
00:42:18,873 --> 00:42:22,389
Search results. "Detective Murphy
making a speedy recovery."

473
00:42:22,389 --> 00:42:24,667
"Alex Murphy. Man or Machine?"

474
00:42:24,667 --> 00:42:28,233
"Thrust into the spotlight,
Clara Murphy won't talk. "

475
00:42:29,065 --> 00:42:31,901
Antoine Vallon.
Search results.

476
00:42:31,901 --> 00:42:36,137
"DPD says Antoine Vallon no longer
suspect due to lack of evidence."

